    "title": "Holy Sonnets: Batter my heart, three-person'd God",
    "maker": "John Donne",
    "date": "1633 (Poems, by J. D., published posthumously)",
    "poem": "Batter my heart, three-person'd God, for you\nAs yet but knock, breathe, shine, and seek to mend;\nThat I may rise and stand, o'erthrow me, and bend\nYour force to break, blow, burn, and make me new.\nI, like an usurp'd town to another due,\nLabor to admit you, but oh, to no end;\nReason, your viceroy in me, me should defend,\nBut is captiv'd, and proves weak or untrue.\nYet dearly I love you, and would be lov'd fain,\nBut am betroth'd unto your enemy;\nDivorce me, untie or break that knot again,\nTake me to you, imprison me, for I,\nExcept you enthrall me, never shall be free,\nNor ever chaste, except you ravish me.",
    "poemNote": "whole",
    "deeper": "He asks to be broken rather than mended. Say why you think he does, and whether you would ask the same.",
    "story": [
     "Donne probably wrote the Holy Sonnets around 1609–10, in his late thirties and without a settled post. After his secret marriage to Anne More in 1601 he had been dismissed as secretary to Lord Keeper Egerton and jailed in the Fleet, and he lived afterwards on patrons and legal work while twelve children were born in sixteen years. Around 1610 he was writing <i>Pseudo-Martyr</i>, an argument against the Catholicism of his upbringing.",
     "None of the Holy Sonnets was printed in his lifetime. They circulated in manuscript, and in the Westmoreland manuscript of about 1620 “Batter my heart” is sixteenth of nineteen. It reached print in <i>Poems, by J. D.</i> (1633), two years after his death, as number ten; the familiar fourteen is Herbert Grierson's of 1912. Donne had by then been ordained, in 1615 at King James's urging, and had died Dean of St Paul's.",
     "The last line is the one most quoted. The opening travelled further. Replying to General Groves about why the 1945 test was named Trinity, J. Robert Oppenheimer said the reason was not clear, named Donne, and added that in another, better known devotional poem Donne opens: batter my heart, three person'd God."
    ],
